\subsubsection{Computing the spreadsheet's filename}

Every ingredient of either filename is already in hand inside the loop: the
username, the Canvas user we just resolved, the matched [[courses]], the
[[--period]] option and the contract's resolved [[start]].  So the naming is
a pure function of those, which also makes it testable without driving the
whole command.
<<helper functions>>=
def amanuensis_xlsx_filename(fmt, user, sheet_user, courses, period, start):
  """
  Returns the filename for `user`'s planning spreadsheet in format `fmt`
  (an `XlsxFormat`).

  `sheet_user` is the Canvas user (or the bare username if Canvas was
  unavailable), `courses` the matched courses (a mapping keyed by
  (course, register)), `period` the `--period` option (or None) and
  `start` the contract's start date.
  """
  if fmt == XlsxFormat.TCS:
    <<let [[parts]] be the TCS filename parts>>
    return "_".join(part for part in parts if part) + ".xlsx"
  return f"{user}-{datetime.datetime.now():%Y-%m-%dT%H%M%S}.xlsx"
@

The TCS format has three parts.
We skip any part that came out empty---a TA we could not find in Canvas has
no last name to give, and a contract that falls outside every configured
period has no period---rather than leave a double underscore behind; a
filename with a missing part is still a usable filename.
<<let [[parts]] be the TCS filename parts>>=
first, last = split_canvas_name(sheet_user, user)
parts = [first, last,
         course_part(courses),
         period_part(period, start)]
@

\paragraph{The name}

The format wants the first and last names separately, but the planning
sheet never consults LADOK (which has them as separate fields), only
Canvas.
Canvas gives us [[sortable_name]], which is \enquote{Last, First}---the same
field [[canvaslms]] itself splits to get at the two halves---so we split on
the first comma.
Without it we fall back to splitting the display [[name]] at its last space,
which is right for the common single-surname case, and finally to the bare
username when Canvas gave us nothing at all (in which case [[sheet_user]] is
the username string).
A multi-word first name or surname would otherwise put a space in the
filename, so we join such words with a hyphen; a slash would split the
filename into directories, so it gets the same treatment.
<<helper functions>>=
def split_canvas_name(sheet_user, fallback):
  """
  Returns (first, last) names of the Canvas user `sheet_user`.

  Falls back to (`fallback`, "") when no name is available.
  """
  sortable = getattr(sheet_user, "sortable_name", None)
  if sortable and "," in sortable:
    last, first = sortable.split(",", 1)
  else:
    name = getattr(sheet_user, "name", None) or fallback
    first, _, last = name.rpartition(" ")
    if not first:
      first, last = last, ""
  return _filename_part(first), _filename_part(last)

def _filename_part(text):
  return "-".join(text.replace("/", "-").split())
@

Let's check the three sources in turn, and that the result is always a
single path component.
<<test functions>>=
def test_split_canvas_name_prefers_sortable_name():
  class User:
    sortable_name = "Riese, Emma"
    name = "Emma Riese"
  assert split_canvas_name(User(), "emmar") == ("Emma", "Riese")

def test_split_canvas_name_falls_back_to_name():
  class User:
    name = "Anna Maria van Dyke"
  assert split_canvas_name(User(), "annam") == ("Anna-Maria-van", "Dyke")

def test_split_canvas_name_falls_back_to_username():
  assert split_canvas_name("alice", "alice") == ("alice", "")

def test_split_canvas_name_keeps_single_path_component():
  class User:
    sortable_name = "de la Cruz, Ana Lucía/Rosa"
  first, last = split_canvas_name(User(), "ana")
  assert first == "Ana-Lucía-Rosa"
  assert last == "de-la-Cruz"
@

\paragraph{The course}

The course part is the course's own identifier in nytid, like [[prgwv26]],
which already carries both the short code and the course instance.
When the regex matched several courses there is no single identifier, so we
join them with [[+]], sorted so the same selection always gives the same
filename.
We take the names from the matched [[courses]] rather than from the
[[course_regex]] argument: a regex like [[.*]] says nothing about which
course the sheet is for.
<<helper functions>>=
def course_part(courses):
  """
  Returns the course part of the TCS filename: the matched course
  identifiers, sorted and joined with `+`.
  """
  return "+".join(sorted({course for course, _ in courses}))
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_course_part_single_course():
  assert course_part({("prgwv26", "reg"): None}) == "prgwv26"

def test_course_part_joins_sorted_courses():
  courses = {("prgm26", "reg"): None, ("prgi26", "reg"): None,
             ("prgi26", "other"): None}
  assert course_part(courses) == "prgi26+prgm26"
@

\paragraph{The period}

The format's last part is the study period, [[P4]].
If the contract was created with [[--period]], that is the period.
Otherwise we find it by date: the configured period
(\cref{sec:periods}) whose dates contain the contract's start.
We use the resolved [[start]]---after [[--set-start]] and [[--push-start]]
have been applied---because that is the date the contract actually starts,
which is what determines the period it belongs to.
<<helper functions>>=
def period_part(period, start):
  """
  Returns the period part of the TCS filename for a contract starting at
  `start`, or "" if no period is known.

  `period` is the `--period` option (an Enum member) or None.
  """
  if period:
    name = period.value
  else:
    name = period_for_date(start.date())
  if not name:
    logging.warning(f"No period contains {start.date()}, "
                    "leaving the period out of the filename.")
    return ""
  return period_label(name)
@

Our periods can be named anything, but the department wants [[P]] and a
number.
Names like [[25/26-1]] or [[P1]] end in the number we need, so we turn
those into [[P1]]; a name that does not end in a number after a separator,
like [[VT25]], we leave as it is---better a recognisable name than a
made-up number.
<<helper functions>>=
def period_label(name):
  """
  Returns the TCS label (`P` + number) of the period named `name`, or
  `name` itself if it doesn't end in a number.
  """
  match = re.search(r"(?:^P|[-/_ ])(\d+)$", name)
  if match:
    return f"P{match.group(1)}"
  return name
@

Finding the period by date is the inverse of [[get_period]]: we walk all
configured periods and pick the first whose range contains the date.
Periods are not supposed to overlap, so the first match is the match.
<<helper functions>>=
def period_for_date(date):
  """
  Returns the name of the configured period containing `date`, or None.
  """
  for name, dates in get_all_periods().items():
    start = datetime.date.fromisoformat(dates["start"])
    end = datetime.date.fromisoformat(dates["end"])
    if start <= date <= end:
      return name
  return None
@

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("name,label", [
  ("25/26-1", "P1"),
  ("25/26-4", "P4"),
  ("P4", "P4"),
  ("VT25", "VT25"),
])
def test_period_label(name, label):
  assert period_label(name) == label

def test_period_for_date(monkeypatch):
